WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.319
With its focus on technology, low-stakes storyline,
and relatively graphic PG-13 violence, Bloodshot

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.160
is a very different superhero flick.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.250
But there's one area in which director Dave
Wilson was clearly inspired by Bloodshot's

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.560
higher-profile peers: the Easter eggs.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.440
In Bloodshot, the namesake hero comes by his
name rather simply.

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:20.999
The top-secret program that created him is
simply called...

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.120
Project Bloodshot.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.100
Hey, it fits.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.660
Not only has Ray Garrison's blood been replaced
by an army of nanites, giving him superpowers,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.020
but he really likes to shoot people.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.860
That's also simple, but it works.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.330
In the comics, however, the organization that
gives Ray his powers goes by a different name.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.980
While one reboot calls the clandestine organization
Project Lazarus, it's usually known as Project

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:40.780
Rising Spirit.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.000
In the first volume of Bloodshot, Project
Rising Spirit was a secret group based in

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:45.500
Japan.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.700
In more recent interpretations, it's a contracting
group that often works with the U.S. military

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.420
and has ties to the Sect, a secret society
so big that includes almost every other secret

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.420
society on Earth.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.300
Well, you know how, in the movie, the company
behind Bloodshot is called RST?

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.100
That's short for Rising Spirit Tech.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.460
Sure, Bloodshot the movie made a few adjustments
to the setup - the company is now run by Guy

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.450
Pearce's Emil Harting, for example, and it
does a lot more work with prosthetics than

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.680
it used to - but this is still the Rising
Spirit fans know and love.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.440
Early in Bloodshot, before he realizes that
everything he knows is a lie, Ray Garrison

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.240
is dead set on taking down Martin Axe, the
man who he thinks killed his wife.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.680
"He can't get in, can he?"

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:23.260
"No chance."

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:25.800
"Oh man."

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.200
If you heard that name and thought it sounded
like something straight out of a '90s comic

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:35.619
book, well, you were right: Martin Axe (or,
often, just "Ax") has been around since 1992,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.840
making him even older than Bloodshot himself.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.880
In the comics, Ax isn't a sadistic terrorist
or a former RST employee.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.820
He's actually more of a computer nerd.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.320
Originally, Ax was a psiot - an evolved human
with psychic powers - who could communicate

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.220
with machines.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.000
On his debut, Ax was a straight-up villain
who was obsessed with getting his hands on

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.160
all the groundbreaking tech he could find,
including Bloodshot's nanites, which he briefly

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:57.660
stole.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.960
Later versions of the character have come
down a little more on the side of a well-meaning

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:01.460
rebel.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.820
He's out to stop high-level corruption, not
steal machines, and often helps out the Renegades,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.920
a group of rebel psiots who fight against
the Harbinger Foundation.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.660
He also often goes by "@x," because this is
still a Valiant Comics property we're talking

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:14.160
about here.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.460
'90s-style silliness is just part of the deal.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.180
In Bloodshot the movie, Bloodshot looks like
Vin Diesel.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.280
Handsome, bald, ripped.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.060
You know the look.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.700
Diesel is striking, but he's still a normal
guy.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.980
In the comics, however, Bloodshot is anything
but.

00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:31.280
Not only does he have hair, but his skin is
white, his eyes are red, and more often than

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.040
not he doesn't wear a shirt, showing off the
giant red circle on his chest.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.460
Canonically, the mark is kind of like a big
scar.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.440
As seen in the comic Rai #0, Rising Spirit
pumped the nanites into Bloodshot's body using

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:44.400
a giant device stuffed with needles.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.140
The circle is basically the mark that the
machine leaves behind.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:51.400
An identical device is used in the film, too,
but it doesn't leave a mark, probably because

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.320
it would look a little silly in live action.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.900
Bloodshot pays tribute to the hero's iconic
look in a few different ways, though.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.700
The way that Diesel's chest starts to glow
red as the nanites in his body heat up is

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.740
the most obvious, but it's not the only one.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.900
Even more subtly, whenever there's a scene
with Ray on RST's operating table, it opens

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:07.900
with a shot from above.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.220
If you pay attention, you'll notice a circular
medical device on Diesel's chest, right where

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.680
that red circle should've been.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.120
Believe it or not, but "man without memories
who is repeatedly manipulated into killing

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:21.269
the people framed for killing his wife" is
not an original concept.

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:25.359
Twenty years before Bloodshot applied that
idea to a superhero story, a young director

00:03:25.359 --> 00:03:29.480
named Christopher Nolan turned out a taut
crime thriller called Memento that dealt with

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.620
the exact same subject.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:31.840
"Stay focused.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:33.860
Find a pen.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:35.780
Gotta write this down."

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.160
"Gotta write it down."

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.900
The Bloodshot parallels are obvious, and they're
not accidental.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.440
Director Dave Wilson admitted that Memento
was a major influence on Bloodshot in an interview

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.780
with The Beat, but you don't have to do much
research to figure that out.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:54.019
Just look at Bloodshot's cast list: Guy Pearce,
who starred as Leonard in Memento, is Bloodshot's

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:54.580
main villain.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.560
Not that the movies are connected or anything,
but there's no way that's just a coincidence.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.780
In terms of tone and aesthetics, Bloodshot
feels a whole lot like Total Recall, Terminator,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.519
and RoboCop, but it only calls out one '80s
hit directly: the beloved Eddie Murphy vehicle

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.540
Coming to America.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.360
In the 1988 comedy, Murphy plays Akeem, crown
prince of the fictional country of Zamunda,

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.940
who travels to New York City to find a bride
who loves him for himself, not his royal title.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.610
In order to pull off the ruse, Akeem and his
friend Semmi pose as poor students and take

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:23.810
working class jobs.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:25.420
Naturally, hilarity ensues.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.500
"I have recently been placed in charge of
garbage.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.360
Do you have any that requires disposal?"

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.340
Anyway, that's what Bloodshot's sidekick Wilfred
Wigans is referring to when he sarcastically

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.440
calls himself the "crown prince of Zamunda."

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.690
Oh, and by the way: while Wilfred mentions
that he has a very comic book-sounding name,

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.170
which implies that Wilfred is some kind of
reference himself, he's actually an original

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.100
character who was created just for the film.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.100
Sorry, Easter egg hunters.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.940
You're going to have to look a little harder.

